[{:db/id 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af7a572f-0636-5725-9441-ef6582928ab9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-af7a572f-0636-5725-9441-ef6582928ab9" :question/id #uuid "af7a572f-0636-5725-9441-ef6582928ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125729" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-4,1)$ and $B(0,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-2)-f(-4,1) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}-2(-2)^{3})-((-4)^{2}-2(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16-16+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf3ea97d-4f26-5777-a472-6a585002f0c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf3ea97d-4f26-5777-a472-6a585002f0c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3ea97d-4f26-5777-a472-6a585002f0c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0ca885d-c4f1-58d6-b2f7-daed73d87732" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a818aab-75ac-5369-aa04-7b5968c78bdd" "answer-aa971f18-d59c-541a-a8c1-bc17ca502230" "answer-b0ca885d-c4f1-58d6-b2f7-daed73d87732" "answer-80844163-7141-53d5-b539-4be5b4478368" "answer-5b414